module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { NoRungStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_claim_run_never_reaches_its_first_verdict_because_its_floor_is_a_whole_tree_closure" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "**a claim run whose claim reads a derived roster stalls before it prints any verdict, and looks healthy while it does**. INVALID STATE: `gunbc run --claim-run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry src/v2/test/claim/derived_roster_seed_agreement.dag --function the_native_rung_drop_roster_agrees_with_the_seeds` is expected to print a verdict line per test fn and exit 0 or 1. Measured twice on two heads: it prints 65 lines -- the census header and the Filesystem.Read counter -- and then prints NOTHING for the remaining 33 and 35 minutes of two capped runs (cut at 64 and 65 minutes). No error, no cause line, no panic, no progress line. RSS holds at 13.8-14.4 GB at 99.9% CPU the whole time. A reader holding a wall clock cannot distinguish 'slow' from 'hung' without instrumenting the read counter and the RSS from outside the process, which is the only reason either observation exists.",

    "WHERE IT STALLS. Both runs froze at exactly 38 Filesystem.Reads, the last file read being `src/v2/compiler/body_lowering_fold.dag`, after which that fold is entered and never returns. The freeze point is identical on main and on a branch whose only difference is DATA: diffing the two instrumented logs leaves 32 lines and every one of them is a path or a timestamp. `body_lowering_fold` is therefore where the run goes to die, not what the claim is about -- the claim asks whether the native derived roster agrees with the seed's.",

    "THE 2085-vs-2172 RECEIPT (2026-11-17; main `dd14ed00b63` with a binary built there, branch `25db57797eb` with a binary built there, same host). The run's own census header prints the size of what it is about to typecheck: `closure_files: 2085` on main and `closure_files: 2172` on the branch -- **+87 files, bought by six added data-only row files** and the imports they pull in. `claim_batch` prints a whole-tree typecheck floor of roughly 1100 modules at 2-3 s each before it prints a single verdict, so the floor alone runs 40-70+ minutes on these trees. That is the class: the price a verdict pays is not the price of the claim, it is the price of the tree, and it rises with every row ANY lane files ANYWHERE -- including rows the claim can never see. The static import closure of `gunbc.rung_drop.standing` is 237 modules and contains ZERO `gunbc.integration_concern` modules, and the claim that stalls is the rung_drop pairing.",

    "THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS A CLASS AND NOT A HANG. The sibling pairing in the same entry file, same binary, same tree, same floor -- `the_native_integration_concern_roster_agrees_with_the_seeds` -- prints its verdict and exits 0 in 112 s at 74 row files and 113 s at 75. So on one tree one claim answers in under two minutes and the other never answers, and nothing the run prints tells you which of the two you are waiting for. A lane that cannot tell these apart re-runs the battery and burns hours: this was measured only because a battery re-run was capped and instrumented.",

    "THE RUNG, AND WHY IT IS UNREAD. No workflow runs this claim: grep over `.github/workflows` at main `7f27f475982` finds no reference to `derived_roster_seed_agreement` and none to `src/v2/test/claim` at all, so CI holds no observation of the stall and no observation of the ~14 GB that came with it. Both measurements above are hand runs on a single shared host. `RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated }` and not a `RungRead`, for two reasons: no receipt in the corpus states a rung for this class, and the ledger deliberately carries no 'not yet measured' arm -- `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode`'s own comment on `UnreadCause` says an arm for unmigrated rows would let a later row be authored unread. 'We timed it once by hand' is a receipt, not a rung.",

    "THE FAMILY HAS ONE RECORDED ANCESTOR, ON THIS VERY CLAIM. gunbc#15258, recorded at `dag/std/algebra.dag` beside `ZipEqWalk`: the consumer that showed up host-side was `v2.test.claim.derived_roster_seed_agreement rung_drop_standing_acquires_with_its_derived_roster` aborting on the host stack, and the trace located it by climbing a fixed step per sibling -- 'the depth was a property of the list's length, not of its contents'. That instance was a cost that scaled with the size of a LIST; this one is a cost that scales with the size of the TREE. Same claim, same shape of mistake: a check whose price is set by something it does not need and does not declare.",

    "CEILING AND TRIGGER. Ceiling: mechanically preventable. Whether a verdict-bearing check pays for the whole tree before its first verdict is a property of the fold that walks the closure, not of any author's diligence, so a bound is mechanical: a per-claim closure instead of a whole-tree one, a cached closure keyed on the tree, or a refusal when the closure exceeds a ceiling the claim declares. Trigger capability, not yet built: the capability for a claim run to STATE, before it starts, the closure it is about to typecheck, and to refuse or bound a run whose closure is not the one the claim needs. Until that exists, every lane's row-file pull request lengthens a floor nobody is watching, and the next instance of this class will again be found only by whoever happens to cap a run and instrument its reads.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
